::ALG2_U05_L02_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.3
Question:
What boundary style is used for y < 2x + 1?
{
=A. Dashed#Correct. Strict inequalities use dashed boundaries because equality is not included.
~B. Solid#Solid boundaries include equality.
~C. No boundary#A graph needs the related boundary line.
~D. Only a point#An inequality has a region, not one point.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.3
Question:
What boundary style is used for y >\= -x + 3?
{
~A. Dashed#Dashed means equality is not included.
=B. Solid#Correct. The symbol >\= includes equality, so the boundary is included.
~C. No boundary#The related equation is still graphed.
~D. Only the y-intercept#The full boundary line is needed.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.3
Question:
Which point satisfies y <\= x + 2?
{
~A. (1,5)#5 <\= 3 is false.
~B. (0,4)#4 <\= 2 is false.
=C. (1,3)#Correct. Substitute to get 3 <\= 3, which is true.
~D. (-1,4)#4 <\= 1 is false.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.3
Question:
For y > x - 1, which side is shaded?
{
~A. Below the line#Below would match less than.
~B. Only on the line#Strict inequality excludes the boundary line.
~C. Neither side#Every inequality shades a half-plane.
=D. Above the line#Correct. Greater than means y-values above the boundary.
}

::ALG2_U05_L02_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.3
Question:
What is a feasible region?
{
=A. The overlap of all solution regions#Correct. A system solution must satisfy every inequality.
~B. Only the first shaded region#All constraints must be used.
~C. Only the boundary line#The feasible region is usually an area.
~D. Any point on the coordinate plane#Not every point satisfies the constraints.
}

